## What We Do, How We Do It, and Why

### What We Do

GokoWeb is the **entire digital backbone** of Goko Hostel. It replaces what would normally be 5-6 separate SaaS products:

| Function | What GokoWeb replaces |
|----------|-----------------------|
| Marketing website | Squarespace / WordPress |
| Guest check-in + ID verification | Paper registers + manual FRRO forms |
| Food ordering + kitchen management | Zomato-like POS / pen-and-paper tickets |
| Expense tracking + daily accounting | Tally / spreadsheet bookkeeping |
| Bed/room management | Hostel PMS (Beds24, Cloudbeds) |
| Booking aggregation | Manually checking OTA emails |

Everything runs on a **single codebase, single deployment, single database** — no microservices, no separate backends, no third-party dashboards staff need to learn.

### How We Do It

**Single-stack monolith on Cloudflare edge:**

- **One Next.js app** serves marketing pages (SSG), guest-facing tools (CSR), and the full admin panel (CSR) — all from the same deployment.
- **One SQLite database** (Cloudflare D1) stores everything — guests, beds, food orders, expenses, settings. No external DB, no Redis, no message queues.
- **Action-based APIs** — every admin endpoint is a single POST route with an `action` field that dispatches to the right handler. This avoids REST route proliferation and keeps the API surface small (23 routes handle 100+ operations).
- **Password-per-request auth** — no JWT tokens, no sessions, no cookies. The password is sent with every API call. Simple to implement, works everywhere (web, curl, future mobile app), and avoids token refresh complexity.
- **Google Drive as file storage** — ID photos, visa documents, and bill receipts go to Drive with public viewer links. No S3, no Cloudflare R2. Drive gives free storage with a familiar admin interface for manual access.
- **Google Vision as AI layer** — OCR for ID validation, passport parsing, visa extraction. No custom ML models. Pay-per-use, high accuracy, supports 100+ languages.

**Frontend patterns:**

- All admin components are **client-side rendered** (`"use client"`) — the admin panel is a SPA within Next.js. No server components, no streaming, no suspense boundaries. This keeps the admin simple and predictable.
- **No global state management** — no Redux, no Zustand. Each admin component manages its own state via `useState`/`useCallback` and loads its own data on mount. The only Context provider is `BookingGateProvider` for the public booking flow — admin components are fully self-contained.
- **Inline tab rendering** — no shared Tab component abstraction. Each admin page defines its own tab array and renders buttons inline. This sounds like duplication but avoids premature abstraction — each page's tabs have different permission logic, styling variants, and mobile behavior.
- **localStorage for guest state** — food cart and phone number persist across page reloads without any backend session. Simple, works offline, no auth needed for guest-facing pages.

### Why We Made These Choices

**Why Cloudflare Workers + D1 (not Vercel + Postgres)?**
- Edge deployment gives <50ms response times globally — guests in India, Europe, and Australia all get fast check-in forms.
- D1 (SQLite) is simpler than Postgres — no connection pooling, no pgbouncer, no cold starts. For a single-hostel app doing <100 concurrent users, SQLite is more than sufficient.
- Cloudflare's free tier is generous — the hostel runs this at near-zero infrastructure cost.

**Why action-based POST (not REST)?**
- A single `/api/admin/checkins` handles 40+ operations. With REST, that would be 40+ route files. The action pattern keeps related operations together, makes auth a single check at the top, and simplifies the frontend API layer (one `fetch` wrapper per domain).
- Trade-off: not cacheable by CDN, not auto-documented by OpenAPI. Acceptable for an internal admin tool.

**Why password-per-request (not JWT/sessions)?**
- The hostel has 3-5 staff members. Session management, token refresh, and logout flows add complexity for zero practical benefit at this scale.
- The password is stored in component state on the admin page — page refresh requires re-login, which is acceptable.
- Future mobile app: same pattern works — store password in secure storage, send with every request.

**Why Google Drive for file storage (not R2/S3)?**
- Staff can browse uploaded IDs directly in Drive's web UI without needing admin panel access.
- Drive's 15GB free tier is sufficient for a single hostel's ID photos.
- Organized into monthly subfolders automatically.
- Trade-off: slower uploads than R2, dependent on Google OAuth tokens. Acceptable.

**Why Vision API for ID validation (not a custom model)?**
- Google Vision handles Aadhaar (Hindi + English), driving licenses (state variations), passports (MRZ), and visas from 100+ countries out of the box.
- No training data needed, no model hosting, no GPU costs.
- Pay-per-use: ~₹1 per validation. The hostel does 5-15 check-ins per day.

**Why amounts in paise (not rupees as float)?**
- Floating-point arithmetic causes rounding errors (₹10.10 + ₹10.20 ≠ ₹20.30 in IEEE 754).
- Integer arithmetic is exact. ₹500.50 = `50050` paise. All display formatting happens at the UI layer.
- This is the same pattern used by Stripe, Razorpay, and every serious payment system.

**Why no shared component library / design system?**
- The admin panel is internal tooling for 3-5 staff. Visual polish matters less than shipping features fast.
- Components use Tailwind utilities directly with brand color tokens (`brand-green`, `brand-sand`, `brand-mist`). Consistent enough without the overhead of a Storybook-driven design system.
- shadcn/ui provides the base primitives (Button, Input, Dialog) — everything else is purpose-built.

**Why no tests?**
- This is a single-hostel internal tool with a single developer. The review agent workflow (3 parallel agents: backend review + frontend review + mock workflow validation) catches bugs before push.
- Build verification (`npx next build`) catches type errors and import issues.
- Trade-off: no regression protection. Acceptable at current scale — if the hostel grows to a chain, automated tests become worthwhile.

**Why XLSX for bulk imports (not CSV)?**
- Excel preserves data types — phone numbers don't lose leading zeros, dates don't get mangled.
- The `xlsx` library handles Excel serial date numbers, which are a common source of bugs with CSV date imports.
- Staff are more familiar with Excel than CSV.

**Why no real-time WebSocket (food orders use polling)?**
- Cloudflare Workers don't natively support WebSocket persistence across requests (Durable Objects would, but add complexity).
- 5-second polling for kitchen, 10-second for guest status — good enough for a 20-table hostel cafe.
- Trade-off: slight delay in order updates. Not noticeable in practice.

